Sorry, but that's not accurate. A "team" is a singular entity, even
though it is made up of several people. It should take a singular
verb.

As a parallel example, take "soccer team" instead of "DevTeam". You
wouldn't say, "My soccer team are in the playoffs this year." Instead,
you say, "My soccer team *IS* in the playoffs this year."

Or take this example. You could say, "Each of the police carries a
gun." (I.e. each person in the police force carries a gun.) However,
you wouldn't say, "each of the team owns a soccer ball" to mean that
every player on the team has a soccer ball. (You could say, "each of
the team MEMBERS owns a soccer ball," but that's different.)

I think if you can precede a word by "a", it is a singular entity.
Thus you can say "a team", "a group," "a bunch", etc., but not "a
police." (You'd have to say "a police force" instead). In other words,
you were quite right about how to use the word "police", but wrong
about why - it's not actually a singular entity. Instead, it's a
plural that doesn't *look* plural, and is hence confusing. (Try
substituting "police officers", an *obvious* plural, everywhere
"police" occurs. If it works, "police" is also plural.)

